Pardus pcs 1
verbosity 1
gui_update_period 0
###############################################################################
#                                                                             #
#  pcs_throughput.pcs                                                         #
#                                                                             #
#  An instrument, not an application: it measures the sustained execution     #
#  rate of the PCS interpreter, in interpreted lines per second, separately   #
#  for the five classes of operation that dominate scientific code.           #
#                                                                             #
#  Why this exists                                                            #
#  --------------                                                             #
#  Every proposal in the accompanying document carries a cost estimate of     #
#  the form  T = L / R , where L is the number of interpreted lines the       #
#  algorithm requires (a quantity that can be counted exactly from the        #
#  algorithm) and R is the interpreter throughput (a quantity that can only   #
#  be measured on the target machine).  This script measures R.  Until it     #
#  has been run, every time estimate in that document is a conjecture.        #
#                                                                             #
#  Convention for counting lines                                              #
#  -----------------------------                                              #
#  A loop iteration is counted as the number of coding lines the interpreter  #
#  must fetch, parse and act upon in one pass of the loop body, INCLUDING     #
#  the terminating endloop (which performs the jump) but excluding the        #
#  loop_while line.  Whether the interpreter re-fetches the loop_while line   #
#  on each pass is an implementation detail of pardclient; if it does, the    #
#  true line count is one greater per iteration than the figure used here     #
#  and the reported rate is therefore a CONSERVATIVE LOWER BOUND, low by      #
#  between 14 and 33 per cent depending on the test.  Costing an algorithm    #
#  with a lower bound on the rate yields an upper bound on the run time,      #
#  which is the direction in which one wishes to be wrong.  The per-iteration #
#  line count for each test is stated in the comment above that test and is   #
#  held in the variable bm_lpi so that the arithmetic is auditable.           #
#                                                                             #
#  Reading the result                                                         #
#  ------------------                                                         #
#  The figure of merit is the microseconds-per-line column.  A modern PC      #
#  running a C interpreter of this design should be expected to fall          #
#  somewhere between 1e5 and 1e7 lines per second; the spread between the     #
#  integer test and the string test indicates how strongly the cost is        #
#  dominated by parsing rather than by arithmetic, which in turn governs      #
#  whether it is worth hand-unrolling the inner loops of a numerical kernel.  #
#                                                                             #
#  Adjust bm_n downwards if a test takes uncomfortably long on your machine;  #
#  the reported rates are normalised and so remain comparable.                #
#                                                                             #
#                                            Prepared for the Prof, 24.09.2026#
###############################################################################

# --- configuration -----------------------------------------------------------
#define BM_ITERATIONS 200000   # iterations per test
#define BM_ARRAYSIZE  1000     # working array length for the indexing test

# --- state -------------------------------------------------------------------
def_var   int    bm_i      0       # loop counter
def_var   int    bm_j      0       # secondary index (array test)
def_var   int    bm_t0     0       # monotonic start stamp
def_var   int    bm_ms     0       # elapsed milliseconds for the test
def_var   int    bm_lpi    0       # interpreted lines per iteration for the test
def_var   float  bm_lines  0.0     # total interpreted lines in the test
def_var   float  bm_rate   0.0     # lines per second
def_var   float  bm_usec   0.0     # microseconds per line
def_var   float  bm_x      0.0     # float scratch
def_var   string bm_s      0.0     # string scratch (defined numeric: see note)
def_var   string bm_msg    _       # console message
def_array float  bm_arr    BM_ARRAYSIZE 1.0

# NOTE on bm_s: the script checker evaluates a string variable's DEFINED value,
# not any value a later set_str gives it.  A string that may ever be used in a
# numerical context must therefore be defined as a number, hence 0.0 and not _.

def_func main
    print_clear
    set_str bm_msg PCS interpreter throughput benchmark
    print_value bm_msg 2
    set_str bm_msg Iterations per test:
    add_var bm_msg Space
    add_var bm_msg BM_ITERATIONS
    print_value bm_msg 2
    set_str bm_msg ----------------------------------------------------------
    print_value bm_msg 2

    # =========================================================================
    # TEST 1 - integer arithmetic
    #   body: add_var, set_condexp, endloop  ->  3 lines per iteration
    # =========================================================================
    set_var bm_lpi 3
    set_var bm_i 0
    set_var bm_t0 Millis
    set_condexp bm_i < BM_ITERATIONS
    loop_while Condexp
        add_var bm_i 1
        set_condexp bm_i < BM_ITERATIONS
    endloop
    milliduration bm_t0 bm_ms
    set_str bm_msg 1. integer add
    call_func bm_report

    # =========================================================================
    # TEST 2 - floating point transcendental via math_var
    #   body: set_var, math_var, add_var, set_condexp, endloop  ->  5 lines
    # =========================================================================
    set_var bm_lpi 5
    set_var bm_i 0
    set_var bm_t0 Millis
    set_condexp bm_i < BM_ITERATIONS
    loop_while Condexp
        set_var bm_x bm_i
        math_var bm_x sqrt
        add_var bm_i 1
        set_condexp bm_i < BM_ITERATIONS
    endloop
    milliduration bm_t0 bm_ms
    set_str bm_msg 2. math_var sqrt
    call_func bm_report

    # =========================================================================
    # TEST 3 - array indexing (the dominant cost in every numerical kernel)
    #   body: set_var, mul_var, set_var, add_var, imod, set_condexp, endloop
    #         ->  7 lines
    # =========================================================================
    set_var bm_lpi 7
    set_var bm_i 0
    set_var bm_j 0
    set_var bm_t0 Millis
    set_condexp bm_i < BM_ITERATIONS
    loop_while Condexp
        set_var bm_x bm_arr[bm_j]
        mul_var bm_x 1.000001
        set_var bm_arr[bm_j] bm_x
        add_var bm_j 1
        math_var bm_j imod BM_ARRAYSIZE
        add_var bm_i 1
        set_condexp bm_i < BM_ITERATIONS
    endloop
    milliduration bm_t0 bm_ms
    set_str bm_msg 3. array read-modify-write
    call_func bm_report

    # =========================================================================
    # TEST 4 - function call overhead
    #   body: call_func, set_condexp, endloop      ->  3 lines
    #   plus inside bm_null: add_var, return       ->  2 lines
    #   ->  5 lines per iteration
    # =========================================================================
    set_var bm_lpi 5
    set_var bm_i 0
    set_var bm_t0 Millis
    set_condexp bm_i < BM_ITERATIONS
    loop_while Condexp
        call_func bm_null
        set_condexp bm_i < BM_ITERATIONS
    endloop
    milliduration bm_t0 bm_ms
    set_str bm_msg 4. call_func round trip
    call_func bm_report

    # =========================================================================
    # TEST 5 - string construction (the cost of formatted output and of any
    #          algorithm that manipulates text, e.g. a FITS header parser)
    #   body: set_str, add_var, add_var, add_var, set_condexp, endloop
    #         ->  6 lines
    # =========================================================================
    set_var bm_lpi 6
    set_var bm_i 0
    set_var bm_t0 Millis
    set_condexp bm_i < BM_ITERATIONS
    loop_while Condexp
        set_str bm_s 0.0
        add_var bm_s bm_i
        add_var bm_s Space
        add_var bm_i 1
        set_condexp bm_i < BM_ITERATIONS
    endloop
    milliduration bm_t0 bm_ms
    set_str bm_msg 5. string build
    call_func bm_report

    set_str bm_msg ----------------------------------------------------------
    print_value bm_msg 2
    set_str bm_msg Use the slowest relevant rate when costing an algorithm.
    print_value bm_msg 2
return

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# bm_null - an empty callee, to isolate the cost of the call itself.
#           Its add_var is the only work it does and is counted above.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
def_func bm_null
    add_var bm_i 1
return

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# bm_report - convert (bm_lpi, bm_ms) into a rate and print one result row.
#             Guards against a zero elapsed time, which would otherwise be a
#             divide-by-zero and terminate the script.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
def_func bm_report
    set_condexp bm_ms < 1
    do_if Condexp
        set_var bm_ms 1              # floor at 1 ms; rate becomes a lower bound
    enddo
    set_var bm_lines BM_ITERATIONS
    mul_var bm_lines bm_lpi          # total interpreted lines executed
    set_var bm_rate bm_lines
    mul_var bm_rate 1000.0           # lines per second = lines * 1000 / ms
    div_var bm_rate bm_ms
    set_var bm_usec 1000000.0
    div_var bm_usec bm_rate          # microseconds per interpreted line

    add_var bm_msg Space
    add_var bm_msg Space
    add_var bm_msg ms:
    add_var bm_msg Space
    add_var bm_msg bm_ms
    add_var bm_msg Space
    add_var bm_msg Space
    add_var bm_msg lines/s:
    add_var bm_msg Space
    add_var bm_msg bm_rate
    add_var bm_msg Space
    add_var bm_msg Space
    add_var bm_msg us/line:
    add_var bm_msg Space
    add_var bm_msg bm_usec
    print_value bm_msg 2
return
exit

###############################################################################
# SCRATCHPAD - ignored by the interpreter
#
# Interpretation of the five tests
# --------------------------------
# T1 gives the floor: the pure fetch-parse-dispatch cost of the simplest
#    possible statement.  Call this rate R1.  No PCS algorithm can run faster
#    than L/R1.
# T2 - T1 isolates the marginal cost of a libm call made through the dynamic
#    dispatch mechanism of math_var.  If this difference is small, trigonometric
#    recurrences (Chebyshev / rotation-by-recurrence) buy little and the clearer
#    direct evaluation should be preferred in a Lomb-Scargle or FFT kernel.
# T3 is the number that actually governs numerical work, because every inner
#    loop of an FFT, a back-projection or an N-body force evaluation is
#    dominated by indexed array traffic rather than by arithmetic.  Use R3 when
#    costing those.
# T4 tells you whether to write a numerical kernel as a function or to inline
#    it.  If the call round trip costs more than a few simple statements, the
#    inner loops of the library headers should be written out in line, at some
#    cost in readability, and the function form kept for the outer levels.
# T5 governs header parsing and report generation only; it is irrelevant to
#    the inner loops but dominates a FITS reader's header phase.
#
# A worked example of the use of these numbers
# --------------------------------------------
# Filtered back-projection of a 256 x 256 slice from 180 projections of 256
# detector bins requires approximately
#     180 * (2 * 25 * (512/2) * log2(512) + 512 * 10)   lines of filtering
#   + 180 * 65536 * 15                                  lines of back-projection
#   = 2.2e7 + 1.8e8  =  2.0e8 interpreted lines.
# At R3 = 1e6 lines/s that is about three and a half minutes; at R3 = 1e5 it is
# about half an hour.  Either is acceptable for an overnight demonstration, but
# the difference decides whether the reconstruction can be shown live.
###############################################################################
